{
 "experiment": "O4.01",
 "title": "Confirm Z3 transferable-feature adaptation on the 160 OOD labels",
 "n": 160,
 "n_good": 138,
 "n_bad": 22,
 "n_factories": 47,
 "n_q1": 60,
 "n_q2": 100,
 "label_source": "human (labels/ood160_labels.json; merge of ood_labels.json q1=60 and ood_labels2.json q2=100, single labeller; 138 good / 22 bad, 47 factories)",
 "protocol": "evaluation only. Per-domain z-score = the target domain's own feature mean/std, computed ONCE on the pooled 160 and sliced by batch (q1/q2 are slices of the pooled score, NOT per-batch z-scores). It is unsupervised (no labels) but TRANSDUCTIVE: a new window cannot be scored without the rest of its batch. Sign from the 150 IndEgo gold windows only. Factory-grouped bootstrap CI (n_boot=2000, seed=0) is the honest CI; window bootstrap is optimistic (correlated within factory).",
 "threshold_rule": "balanced accuracy at a fixed natural threshold: 0 for the z-score combos (the POOLED signed-mean centre), 0.5 for probability heads / frozen ensemble. No OOD threshold tuning. Because 0 is the pooled mean, the z-score BA is NOT deployable without a batch-normalisation rule; treat it as descriptive.",
 "selection_provenance": "q1 (the first 60) informed feature selection and the Z3.03 recipe, so the pooled-160 headline is q1-contaminated; q2 (100 windows, 13 bad) is the only clean hold-out and is NOT separable from chance on its own. O2.03 (m/fewshot160) re-ran the sign table on all 160 labels and changed global_shift (stable -> flip) and frozen_frac (flip -> stable); O4 kept the pre-O2.03 set, and (b2) reports recipe (b) with global_shift dropped.",
